    description: 'Visualize the entire flow from idea to value-delivered. Identify waste (handoffs, queues, rework). The First Way operationalized.',
    visualizations: [
      {
        title: 'Value stream — current vs future state',
        description: 'Map every step from idea to running-in-production: code, review, build, test, deploy. For each: lead time + process time + wait time. The biggest gap is the bottleneck.',
        image: '/diagrams/devops/a5-value-stream.png',
      },
    ],
    introduction: `Value Stream Mapping (VSM) is a Lean-manufacturing technique adapted by Toyota in the 1990s and now standard in software DevOps. Verbatim from The DevOps Handbook (Ch 4): "A value stream is the sequence of activities that an organization undertakes to deliver upon a customer request."

For software, the value stream typically is:
- Idea (product / customer request)
- Design / architecture
- Code
- Code review
- Build
- Test
- Stage
- Deploy
- Run / observe
- Learn / iterate

For each step, VSM measures THREE numbers:
- Lead time (LT): wall-clock time from arriving at the step to leaving it.
- Process time (PT): actual hands-on work time.
- Wait time (WT): time spent waiting (in queues, for approvals, for environments).

The interesting ratio is PT / LT. In most software value streams, this is shockingly low — 5-15%. Meaning 85-95% of lead time is WAITING, not doing.

Examples (industry typical):
- Code review: lead time 2-5 days; process time 30 min. Ratio: 0.5-2%.
- Pre-prod test environment: lead time 4 hours (waiting for environment); process time 15 min (running tests). Ratio: 6%.
- Deploy approval: lead time 24-72 hours; process time 5 min. Ratio: 0.1%.

The First Way of DevOps (Flow) is operationalized by VSM:
1. Map the current state.
2. Identify the largest LT - PT gap (the longest wait).
3. Apply Flow practices to that step.
4. Re-measure. Iterate.

WORKED EXAMPLE (from the Handbook):
A team had 4-month lead time from commit to production. VSM revealed:
- Code (2 weeks)
- Review (3 weeks waiting for senior engineer)
- QA queue (4 weeks waiting for QA team)
- Stage deploy (1 week waiting for env provisioning)
- UAT (2 weeks waiting for product owner)
- Prod approval (3 weeks committee meetings)
- Prod deploy (1 week deploy windows)

The actual "work" in those 4 months was ~3 days. The fix: parallelize reviews, shift QA into the team, automate environment provisioning, reduce approval committees. Result: lead time dropped from 4 months to 3 days.

CONCRETE PRACTICES TO REDUCE LEAD TIME:

For each common bottleneck:

Code review wait: small PRs (< 200 lines), pair-programming-as-review, automated approval for trivial changes (dependencies bumps), review SLAs.

QA wait: shift-left testing (move test ownership into stream-aligned team), automated test suites, no separate QA team for routine changes.

Environment wait: IaC, ephemeral preview environments per PR, GitOps for env management.

Deploy approval: change advisory board → policy-as-code, automatic approval for SLO-compliant changes, exceptions only for risky ones.

Deploy windows: continuous deployment to canary; only the customer-facing release is gated.

VSM TOOLS:

- Whiteboard + sticky notes (low-tech; works for first pass)
- Lucidchart, Mural, Miro (collaborative)
- ConnectALL, LinearB, Faros AI (auto-instrumented from CI/CD + ticketing systems)

For interview prep, you should be able to:
- Walk through the LT / PT / WT framework
- Explain why most software value streams have very low PT/LT ratio
- Apply VSM diagnostically to a hypothetical scenario
- Connect VSM to the First Way and DORA Lead Time metric`,
