        question: 'When does Buildkite win over GitHub Actions for a monorepo?',
        answer: `Buildkite's value compounds with monorepo size. Below ~30 services and ~50 engineers, GHA is usually the right answer. Above ~100 services and ~200 engineers, Buildkite often wins.

The scaling story:

Static pipeline.yml is fine for small monorepos. dorny/paths-filter + matrix + Turborepo gets you 30 services managed.

Past ~50 services, static YAML becomes a maintenance burden. Adding a new service requires editing the workflow. Branching shapes (different test commands per service) require if: chains. Agent capacity allocation can't be dynamic.

Buildkite's dynamic pipeline generation handles this naturally. The generator script reads service config (a single source of truth), emits the right pipeline shape per build. Adding a new service is "create services/foo/buildkite.yml"; generator picks it up automatically.

Case study: Shopify's monorepo. ~3000 services, 5000+ engineers. Pipeline definitions are entirely generated from git diff and service registry. A typical PR's pipeline: ~10-50 jobs targeted at specific agent fleets, queued and executed within minutes. Static YAML for this scale would be unmanageable.

Other Buildkite advantages at scale:

Custom agent fleets per workload. ARM builds on Graviton instances; GPU jobs on g5; large builds on c5.9xlarge; deploys on small instances with deploy IAM. Buildkite makes fleet specialization first-class.

Spot capacity. Build agents on spot save 60-70% vs on-demand. Mega-monorepos run thousands of CI hours daily; the savings are material ($100k+/year for large orgs).

Network access to internal services. Agents on your VPC reach internal databases, private package registries, on-prem dependencies natively. GHA self-hosted runners can do this too but the architecture is "exception"; on Buildkite it's the default.

When GHA still wins despite scale:

You're on GitHub. Integration tax of separate Buildkite is real — separate UI, separate webhook config, less native PR experience.

Cost-conscious. Buildkite + your own infrastructure isn't necessarily cheaper than GHA Pro at every scale. The crossover depends on usage patterns.

Want a polished marketplace. GHA actions ecosystem is significantly broader.

When GHA + ARC (Actions Runner Controller) is enough. ARC gives you self-hosted execution with GHA's orchestration. Closer to Buildkite's hybrid model than people realize. For shops that want self-hosted but stay on GitHub, ARC is the answer most of the time.

Pragmatic 2026 stance: Buildkite is the default for mega-monorepos at companies that have outgrown SaaS-only CI. Shopify, Airbnb, Pinterest, Etsy, Block — these aren't accidents; they're the company size and repo shape that make Buildkite shine. For everyone else, GHA or GitLab CI is the better default.`,
